================================================================================
ENGINEERING DECISION AND INCIDENT LOG
Project: Zero-Trust Amazon EKS with Karpenter
Scope: Private EKS control plane, SSM administrative access, and dynamic capacity
================================================================================

PURPOSE
-------
This log records material implementation decisions, deployment failures, root
causes, and corrective actions encountered while building the platform. It is
intended to preserve operational context for future maintainers and to prevent
known failure modes from being reintroduced.

Environment-specific identifiers, account numbers, endpoint names, and instance
IDs have been replaced with placeholders. Refer to Terraform state and environment
outputs for current values.


ARCHITECTURE BASELINE
---------------------
- The EKS Kubernetes API is private-only.
- Operators access the API through an authenticated SSM port-forwarding session.
- The administration instance accepts no inbound connections and uses no SSH keys.
- A fixed EKS managed node group hosts critical system services and Karpenter.
- Karpenter provisions Bottlerocket workload nodes from Spot and On-Demand capacity.
- Karpenter uses EKS Pod Identity; worker nodes use a separate IAM role.
- EventBridge and SQS deliver interruption and lifecycle events to Karpenter.
- Terraform is organized into reusable modules and isolated environment roots.


DEPLOYMENT MODEL
----------------
The private Kubernetes endpoint creates an intentional bootstrap dependency:
Terraform must create an administrative path before Kubernetes and Helm providers
can reach the cluster.

Phase 1 - AWS foundation
  terraform apply \
    -target=module.vpc \
    -target=module.eks \
    -target=module.karpenter_iam \
    -target=module.bastion

Phase 2 - Kubernetes convergence
  1. Establish the SSM tunnel.
  2. Route the kubeconfig entry through the local tunnel.
  3. Run an un-targeted terraform plan.
  4. Apply the reviewed plan to converge all remaining resources.

Targeted apply is a bootstrap mechanism only. It is not the normal steady-state
deployment workflow and must always be followed by an un-targeted plan and apply.


INCIDENT 01 - EKS VERSION REJECTED DURING CONTROL-PLANE CREATION
================================================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: EKS control plane

Observed behavior
-----------------
AWS returned InvalidParameterException when the requested Kubernetes version was
not available in the selected region or no longer supported by EKS.

Root cause
----------
The configured Kubernetes version was outside the versions accepted by the EKS
CreateCluster API for the target region at deployment time.

Corrective action
-----------------
- Selected an EKS version currently supported in the target region.
- Kept the cluster version configurable at the environment level.
- Added version compatibility to the pre-deployment review checklist.

Engineering note
----------------
An unsupported version normally fails API validation rather than remaining in
CREATING indefinitely. A prolonged CREATING state should instead trigger checks
of service events, IAM, subnets, IP availability, and control-plane networking.


INCIDENT 02 - MANAGED NODE GROUP AMI INCOMPATIBILITY
====================================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: EKS managed node group

Observed error
--------------
  InvalidParameterException: Requested AMI for this Kubernetes version is not
  supported

Root cause
----------
The node group resolved to an AMI type that AWS rejected for the configured EKS
version. The module input also used instance_type instead of the expected
instance_types attribute.

Corrective action
-----------------
- Explicitly set:

    ami_type = "AL2023_x86_64_STANDARD"

- Changed instance_type to instance_types and supplied a list.
- Re-ran terraform plan to confirm the intended node-group replacement/update.

Preventive control
------------------
Pin and test the EKS version, EKS module version, AMI type, and managed add-on
versions as a compatible release set before promotion.


INCIDENT 03 - POD IDENTITY ASSOCIATION CREATED BEFORE THE CLUSTER
=================================================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: Terraform dependency graph / EKS Pod Identity

Observed error
--------------
  CreatePodIdentityAssociation: ResourceNotFoundException:
  No cluster found for name: <CLUSTER_NAME>

Root cause
----------
Terraform could schedule the Pod Identity association before the EKS control
plane became available because the module boundary did not express a sufficient
dependency.

Corrective action
-----------------
- Passed module.eks.cluster_name into the Karpenter IAM module.
- Added an explicit depends_on = [module.eks] where the resource graph still did
  not encode the required ordering.

Preventive control
------------------
Prefer dependencies implied by resource references. Use explicit depends_on at
module boundaries only when a real lifecycle dependency cannot otherwise be
represented.


INCIDENT 04 - HELM PROVIDER COULD NOT REACH THE PRIVATE EKS API
===============================================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: Network access / Terraform providers

Observed error
--------------
  Kubernetes cluster unreachable: dial tcp <PRIVATE_IP>:443: i/o timeout

Root cause
----------
The Helm and Kubernetes providers ran on the operator workstation, while the EKS
endpoint resolved exclusively to private VPC addresses. No network path existed
from the workstation to the control plane.

Corrective action
-----------------
- Adopted the two-phase bootstrap and convergence workflow documented above.
- Established an SSM remote-host port-forwarding session through the administration
  instance before running Kubernetes-dependent Terraform resources.

Validated by
------------
  kubectl cluster-info
  kubectl get nodes -o wide
  terraform plan


INCIDENT 05 - INVALID SSM START-SESSION PARAMETERS
==================================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: AWS CLI / Terraform output formatting

Observed error
--------------
  Error parsing parameter '--parameters': Invalid JSON: Expecting property name
  enclosed in double quotes

Root cause
----------
The generated shell command contained incorrectly escaped JSON. An earlier version
also used postNumber instead of the required portNumber key.

Corrective action
-----------------
- Generated the parameter object with Terraform jsonencode().
- Wrapped the resulting JSON in single quotes for POSIX shells.
- Used the exact SSM document parameter names.

Known-good command shape
------------------------
  aws ssm start-session \
    --region <AWS_REGION> \
    --target <BASTION_INSTANCE_ID> \
    --document-name AWS-StartPortForwardingSessionToRemoteHost \
    --parameters '{"host":["<EKS_ENDPOINT_HOST>"],"portNumber":["443"],"localPortNumber":["8443"]}'


INCIDENT 06 - SSM TARGET NOT CONNECTED
======================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: Administration instance / outbound connectivity

Observed error
--------------
  TargetNotConnected: <BASTION_INSTANCE_ID> is not connected

Root cause
----------
The SSM agent could not establish its outbound control channel to Systems Manager.
The initial network placement did not provide a functional HTTPS path to the SSM,
SSM Messages, and EC2 Messages services.

Corrective action used during development
-----------------------------------------
- Enabled a public IPv4 address on the administration instance.
- Retained zero inbound security-group rules and allowed required outbound HTTPS.
- Recreated the instance and verified its managed-instance status.

Production recommendation
-------------------------
Place the administration instance in a private subnet and provide either:

  a. Controlled NAT egress; or
  b. Interface VPC endpoints for Systems Manager services, plus required supporting
     endpoints and DNS configuration.

A public IP was sufficient for the development recovery, but is not required by
SSM and is not the preferred zero-trust production topology.

Validation
----------
  aws ssm describe-instance-information \
    --region <AWS_REGION> \
    --filters Key=InstanceIds,Values=<BASTION_INSTANCE_ID>


INCIDENT 07 - SESSION MANAGER PLUGIN MISSING LOCALLY
====================================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: Operator workstation

Observed error
--------------
  SessionManagerPlugin is not found

Root cause
----------
AWS CLI v2 was installed, but the Session Manager plugin required by
aws ssm start-session was not available on the workstation.

Corrective action
-----------------
Installed the AWS Session Manager plugin and restarted the shell.

Validation
----------
  session-manager-plugin --version
  aws ssm start-session --help


INCIDENT 08 - TLS HOSTNAME VERIFICATION FAILED THROUGH LOCALHOST
================================================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: TLS / local name resolution

Observed error
--------------
  x509: certificate is valid for <EKS_ENDPOINT_HOST>, not localhost

Root cause
----------
The kubeconfig server was set to https://localhost:8443. The EKS API certificate
contains the AWS-issued cluster endpoint hostname, not localhost, in its subject
alternative names.

Corrective action
-----------------
- Mapped the original EKS endpoint hostname to 127.0.0.1 in /etc/hosts.
- Kept the endpoint hostname in kubeconfig and changed only the port to 8443.

  127.0.0.1 <EKS_ENDPOINT_HOST>
  server: https://<EKS_ENDPOINT_HOST>:8443

Security note
-------------
TLS verification remained enabled. Disabling certificate verification was neither
required nor accepted as a solution.


INCIDENT 09 - KUBECONFIG SILENTLY REVERTED TO PORT 443
======================================================
Status: Resolved
Layer: kubeconfig lifecycle

Observed error
--------------
  The connection to the server <EKS_ENDPOINT_HOST> was refused:
  dial tcp 127.0.0.1:443: connect: connection refused

Root cause
----------
Running aws eks update-kubeconfig rewrote the cluster server URL to the standard
EKS endpoint on port 443. The SSM tunnel was listening locally on port 8443.

Corrective action
-----------------
Reapplied the tunnel-specific endpoint after every update-kubeconfig operation:

  kubectl config set-cluster <CLUSTER_NAME> \
    --server=https://<EKS_ENDPOINT_HOST>:8443

Preventive control
------------------
Use a dedicated kubeconfig context or a small, reviewed helper script for tunneled
access so the standard AWS-generated context remains unchanged.


POST-RECOVERY VALIDATION
------------------------
The following checks establish that the administrative path and core platform are
operational. They do not replace application-level or resilience testing.

  # Control-plane connectivity
  kubectl cluster-info
  kubectl get --raw=/readyz?verbose

  # Fixed system capacity
  kubectl get nodes -l workload.type=system -o wide

  # Karpenter control plane and custom resources
  kubectl get pods -n karpenter
  kubectl get nodepools
  kubectl get ec2nodeclasses
  kubectl get nodeclaims

  # Provisioning activity
  kubectl logs -n karpenter \
    -l app.kubernetes.io/name=karpenter \
    -c controller \
    --follow

  # Capacity characteristics
  kubectl get nodes \
    -L karpenter.sh/capacity-type,kubernetes.io/arch,node.kubernetes.io/instance-type


LESSONS AND PERMANENT CONTROLS
------------------------------
1. Treat private-cluster access as a first-class deployment dependency.
2. Follow every bootstrap target operation with a complete plan and apply.
3. Keep TLS hostname verification intact when tunneling the Kubernetes API.
4. Test EKS, AMI, add-on, Terraform module, and Karpenter versions together.
5. Encode genuine lifecycle dependencies explicitly at module boundaries.
6. Prefer private SSM connectivity through NAT or VPC endpoints in production.
7. Separate system capacity from dynamically provisioned workload capacity.
8. Keep environment-specific identifiers and credentials out of source control.
9. Validate both scale-up and interruption behavior before production promotion.


